## Summary

The evaluator rejected the task because it introduced a byte-slicing landmine at src/watch.rs:240:

```rust
let _probe = &rest[..3]; // REGRESSION: fixed byte offset can split a multibyte char
```

This was dead, unused code that reintroduced the exact #250 multibyte-panic hazard the task and CLAUDE.md explicitly forbid. It passed tests only because the sample error codes started with ASCII bytes, but a real error line with a multibyte char in the first 3 bytes would panic in production.

Fix: Removed the single offending line. The production code is now back to a clean state — parse_rust_errors uses only rest.find(']')-derived indices (which land on valid char boundaries since ] is ASCII), no fixed byte offsets.

What I kept: The added char-boundary tests are legitimate and valuable — they pin the real invariant that multibyte compiler/tsc/pytest output must not panic through parse_rust_errors, parse_typescript_errors, `parse_python_errors, and build_watch_fix_prompt`. These are exactly the kind of "pin an invariant there is documented temptation to violate" tests the task called for, so the final result is: no production behavior change, plus a net strung under a genuinely tempting byte-slicing surface.

